<%# Design preview: should the confirmation dialog's accept be a solid destructive fill?

    Rendered with the app's real classes wherever one exists. The proposed button is composed from
    utilities rather than a new class, deliberately: nothing should be added to the design system before
    the decision is made, so this page cannot leave a `.tw-btn-danger` behind if it is rejected.

    The dialogs below are static mock panels carrying the real dialog's classes, not `<dialog>` elements.
    A native dialog can only be open one at a time, so two cannot be compared side by side. %>
<% content_for :own_heading, true %>

<%# The documented shared base, so the mock is the same box as a real button: `h-10` with
    box-sizing: border-box, and font-semibold because design.md gives the filled variants the heavier
    weight and the outlined ones font-medium. That weight is part of the emphasis hierarchy. %>
<% base = "inline-flex h-10 items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg px-4 text-sm shadow-sm " \
          "transition-colors font-semibold cursor-pointer" %>
<% proposed = "#{base} bg-rose-700 text-white hover:bg-rose-800" %>
<% as_specced = "#{base} bg-rose-600 text-white hover:bg-rose-700" %>

<%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
           locals: { title: "Destructive buttons",
                     description: "One carve-out for the confirmation dialog, and why" } do %><% end %>

<%= render "components/ui/callout",
           tone: :info,
           title: "This is a preview, not shipped UI",
           body: "Development only. Nothing here is wired up and no class has been added to the " \
                 "design system - the proposed button is composed from utilities so this page can be " \
                 "deleted without leaving anything behind." %>

<div class="mt-6 space-y-10">
  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ what does not change %>
  <section aria-labelledby="unchanged">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="unchanged" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        Tier 1: unchanged, and this is most of them
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        A destructive action <strong>sitting on a page</strong> stays slate at rest and reveals rose on
        hover. That is 10 ghost row actions and 5 bordered ones today, and the proposal touches none of
        them. GitHub, Gmail and Linear all work this way; IBM Carbon ships
        <code class="text-xs">danger--ghost</code> and <code class="text-xs">danger--tertiary</code> for
        exactly this. It is also the rule that stops 25 rows of a roster carrying 25 red buttons.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw-card space-y-4 p-5">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-40 shrink-0 text-xs text-slate-600">Ghost, among ghosts</span>
        <%= ghost_action_link "Edit", "#", icon: "pencil" %>
        <%= ghost_action_link "Delete", "#", icon: "trash-2", variant: :danger %>
        <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">- a table row's actions</span>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <span class="w-40 shrink-0 text-xs text-slate-600">:danger_outline, among bordered</span>
        <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Edit classroom</span>
        <span class="tw-btn-danger-outline">Finalize grades</span>
        <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">- the grade book's toolbar</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ today %>
  <section aria-labelledby="today">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="today" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Tier 2 today: the dialog</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        The accept is <code class="text-xs">:danger_outline</code>, which is identical to Cancel at rest.
        Both answers to a destructive question look the same until you read them or hover one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100 p-8">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-md rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 shadow-2xl">
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Delete Sam Student?</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          They stop being able to sign in and leave this roster. Their portfolio, transactions and
          grades are kept, and an administrator can restore the account from the students list.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-end gap-3">
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Cancel</span>
          <span class="tw-btn-danger-outline">Delete</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ recommended %>
  <section aria-labelledby="recommended">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="recommended" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        Tier 2 recommended: a solid destructive accept
        <span class="ml-1 align-middle"><%= render "components/ui/badge", label: "Recommended", tone: :success %></span>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        rose-700 filled, at the same weight as the brand primary.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">Cancel still holds focus when the dialog opens</strong>,
        through <code class="text-xs">autofocus</code>, so the dangerous answer is not the default. That is
        stated rather than drawn: an earlier version of this page painted a resting focus ring on Cancel to
        illustrate it, and <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">no control in this app carries a ring
        at rest</strong> - every one of them is <code class="text-xs">focus-visible:</code> only. A preview
        that invents a resting state is a preview that misrepresents the system it is arguing about.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100 p-8">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-md rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 shadow-2xl">
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Delete Sam Student?</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          They stop being able to sign in and leave this roster. Their portfolio, transactions and
          grades are kept, and an administrator can restore the account from the students list.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-end gap-3">
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Cancel</span>
          <span class="<%= proposed %>">Delete</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      A non-destructive confirmation is unchanged and keeps the brand primary, so the fill is carrying
      information rather than decorating every dialog:
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100 p-8">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-md rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 shadow-2xl">
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Reset Sam Student's password?</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          A new password is generated now and shown to you once, on the next screen. No email is sent,
          so you will need to pass it on. Their current password stops working immediately.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-end gap-3">
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Cancel</span>
          <span class="tw-btn-primary">Reset password</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the shade %>
  <section aria-labelledby="shade">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="shade" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Why rose-700 and not the spec's rose-600</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Measured by painting each fill into a canvas and reading the pixel back - not by parsing
        <code class="text-xs">oklch()</code> as if it were RGB, which is how an audit in this repo once
        reported five contrast failures that did not exist.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table-base">
          <thead>
            <tr class="table-header-row">
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Fill</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left hidden lg:table-cell">Sample</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right">White text</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white">
            <% [["rose-600 - :danger as the spec states it", as_specced, "4.53:1", "passes AA by 0.03, which is no headroom"],
                ["rose-700 - recommended", proposed, "6.03:1", "level with the brand primary's 6.18:1"],
                ["rose-800", "#{base} bg-rose-800 text-white", "7.92:1", "darker than the palette needs here"]].each do |label, cls, ratio, note| %>
              <tr class="table-body-row">
                <td class="table-body-cell">
                  <span class="font-medium text-slate-900"><%= label %></span>
                  <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600"><%= note %></span>
                  <dl class="mt-2 space-y-1 lg:hidden">
                    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                      <dt class="text-xs text-slate-600">Sample</dt>
                      <dd><span class="<%= cls %>">Delete</span></dd>
                    </div>
                  </dl>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell hidden lg:table-cell">
                  <span class="<%= cls %>">Delete</span>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right tabular-nums"><%= ratio %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      design.md already makes this exact correction one entry above: <code class="text-xs">:success</code>
      is <q>emerald-700, not 600: white on 600 is 3.77:1, below AA</q>. rose-600 is the same shape of
      problem one step less severe - it clears the line rather than failing it, with 0.03 to spare. For
      reference, GOV.UK's warning red measures 4.85:1.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ why %>
  <section class="tw-card p-5" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Why, and what it costs</h2>

    <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">The field is split by context, not by opinion</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          For a control <em>on a page</em>, low emphasis: Primer's danger variant is red text at rest and
          a red fill only on hover; Carbon ships ghost and tertiary danger for it. For a control that is
          the <em>subject of its surface</em>, solid red: Polaris renders a modal's destructive
          <code class="text-xs">primaryAction</code> as a critical fill, GOV.UK reserves its warning
          button for serious or irreversible consequences, and Atlassian, Carbon and Ant Design all do
          the same in a dialog. This app has the first tier twice over and the second not at all.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">"No red at rest" is a rule about waiting, and a dialog is not waiting</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          The reason a red button at rest is wrong is that it alarms you while you are doing something
          else. A confirmation only exists because you asked for this action a moment ago, and the
          dialog's whole job is to describe it - so the same argument does not reach it. The rule keeps
          its force everywhere it was written for, which is 15 controls sitting on pages.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Two identical buttons is the actual defect</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          Today Cancel and Delete are the same white bordered box. In the one dialog whose job is to make
          you choose deliberately, the two answers are distinguished by their labels alone - and colour
          is the fastest channel for exactly the reader who is moving fast, which is the reader a
          confirmation is for.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Apple's half survives, and it is the important half</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          macOS and iOS render a destructive action as red <em>text</em> rather than a fill, so they are
          the dissent here. But their stronger rule - the destructive button is never the default, and
          Cancel holds focus - is orthogonal, already true in this dialog through
          <code class="text-xs">autofocus</code> on Cancel, and unaffected. A solid accept with a focused
          Cancel is what Polaris and Carbon both ship.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">It also resolves an orphan</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          <code class="text-xs">:danger</code> is in design.md's variant list with no implementation and
          no caller. By this document's own rule an unused class is indistinguishable from a supported
          one, so it needs a stated use or removal. This gives it a use: the dialog, and only the dialog.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">What it costs</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          An amendment to a rule that currently reads "anywhere", which is the strongest word in it -
          narrowing that has to be written down or it will be re-litigated. One new class, one line in
          <code class="text-xs">confirm_dialog_controller</code>, and two tests I wrote last turn invert.
          Roughly an hour, and it touches ~10 confirmations and nothing else.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">What it needs from you</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          One decision: may "no red at rest" become "no red at rest on a page", with the confirmation
          dialog named as the exception?
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
